    "narrative": "Ghost Ride the WHIP enters today\u2019s slate with a clear mandate: exploit high-upside matchups against Aldegheri and capitalize on prime lineup positioning. **Max Muncy** (67.46) and **Henry Bolte** (65.40) stand as our elite plays, both benefiting from a massive +19% SP skill boost against the favorable Aldegheri. **Henry Bolte** is particularly dangerous, as his leadoff status provides a 10% order bonus and immense potential for counting stats in what projects to be a high-scoring affair. **James Wood** (64.47) remains a top-tier start despite a 30% rain risk; his 16% park factor enhancement and leadoff duties against Bradish make him a high-ceiling anchor, provided he stays on the field.\n\nPlatoon edges are heavy contributors to today\u2019s board, with **Max Muncy** and **Kyle Karros** receiving significant boosts (+7% each) to solidify their roles. **Austin Riley** (61.15) overcomes a sub-optimal park factor with an 11% skill advantage against Ray, while **Josh Naylor** (60.76) holds the floor at 1B by maximizing his #3 hole opportunities. The most difficult benching decision was **Travis Bazzana** (50.14), who narrowly missed the cut behind **Alex Freeland** (50.21). While **Travis Bazzana** carries a solid 8% order bonus, the optimizer favored **Alex Freeland** due to the favorable wind conditions, which added 5% to his efficiency score and ultimately broke the tie.\n\nWe are keeping a close eye on **Jose Caballero** (47.56); his lineup status remains pending, and his score is depressed by a 13% platoon disadvantage against Sonny Gray. We are opting to roll with him for the potential speed infusion, but we have **Zach McKinstry** on standby should the lineup shuffle force a move. While **Corbin Carroll** (57.59) sits slightly below the \"Strong\" threshold due to a difficult park and opposing pitcher matchup, his elite sprint speed remains essential to our 5x5 strategy, necessitating his inclusion despite the marginal efficiency rating. W